[10:36:18] STORY-WEB-012 is complete. I resumed the interrupted attempt rather than restarting it.
[10:36:18] ## What I found
[10:36:18] The previous session had committed the **entire production implementation** plus the Discovery test fixtures in `04d7555` and stopped before writing a single test. I verified that code against the acceptance criteria instead of rewriting it, then added what was missing: the tests, the browser evidence, and the documentation.
[10:36:18] ## Three defects the new tests exposed
[10:36:18] 1. **A reload silently rewrote the inventory character.** `reconcileInventoryCharacter` re-derived the value from the currently selected scope on every selector read whenever the user hadn't explicitly picked one — but a scope change deliberately *doesn't* reconcile. So selecting Chef/Sat Anat then pressing Reload submitted `inventoryCharacterName: "Sat Anat"` where the result on screen had been calculated with `"Nbt Anch"`: a changed calculation input from the one action whose job is to preserve them.
[10:36:18] 2. **The page claimed "no character" before it knew.** The results region tested `!canCalculate` ahead of `isLoading`, and `isLoading` stayed false during the selector read — so first paint asserted "No character with a crafting discipline is available… Synchronize the account" while that read was still in flight. Found because the live check reads the page right after `domcontentloaded`.
[10:36:18] 3. **My live script hid its own failures.** Unawaited `waitForResponse` promises became uncaught rejections on browser close, replacing the real assertion message with an error pointing at a setup line. Fixed, and recorded in `tasks/lessons.md` since it cost a full debug cycle.
[10:36:18] ## Evidence
[10:36:18] | Check | Result |
[10:36:18] |---|---|
[10:36:18] | `CraftingDiscoveryScreen.spec.ts` (new) | 33 passed |
[10:36:18] | `discoveryResolutionAssociation.spec.ts` (new) | 23 passed |
[10:36:18] | `vitest run src/crafting src/__tests__` | 12 files, 219 passed |
[10:36:18] | `vitest run` (whole frontend) | 19 files, 289 passed |
[10:36:18] | type-check / build | clean, ✓ 642ms |
[10:36:18] | `smoke:discovery` (new, stub origin) | PASSED, 13 steps, real Chrome |
[10:36:18] | `smoke:discovery:live` (new, real backend) | PASSED |
[10:36:18] The live run is the strongest evidence: against the real backend on 8080 with real data, **672 rendered rows matched the response field for field**, and **637 of them had non-positive profit and every one was still listed** — direct proof that Profit's "hide profit ≤ 0" filter was not imported as Discovery eligibility. Recipe 291 resolved to a real 4-node tree matching the backend's depth-first order.
[10:36:18] One baseline failure existed before I started: `App.spec.ts` asserted the exact navigation list from *before* this story added `#/discovery` — the `lessons.md` "a check that asserts an absence expires the day the feature arrives" pattern.
[10:36:18] ## Limits I want to be explicit about
[10:36:18] - The eligibility rules themselves (rating filter, account-wide knowledge, normal-discovery) are backend-owned and **were not re-verified**. Every special state — blocked, no-result, null-vs-zero, mismatched echo, 404, `RESULT_UNAVAILABLE` — is **controlled-response evidence only**.
[10:36:18] - **No §33 timing is claimed.** Discovery has no display limit and no virtualization, so those 672 live candidates put 672 rows in the DOM. I recorded that in `CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE.md` §5.11 rather than measuring it.
[10:36:18] - Criterion 8 (icons) is **met, not deferred** — WEB-010 is DONE, so `ItemIcon` is reused with the backend's `iconUrl` verbatim. Nothing outstanding to record.
[10:36:18] I started a dev server on isolated port 5193 for the live check and stopped it afterwards; `TaskStop` left the `vite` child listening, so I killed it by command line and **confirmed port release with a failed `curl`**. The pre-existing backend on 8080 is untouched. Nothing under `src/` or `pom.xml` changed, and I did not commit — per `CODING_GUIDELINES.md` §8 that's the orchestrator's step.
